WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Short-term memory.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:05.190
An agent's working memory is its context window.

3
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.100
Here is how to keep it healthy.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.733
An agent's short term memory is simply the context window.

5
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:16.667
The history is re-sent on every call, so you pay for every token, every time.

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:22.100
And models get measurably worse at using facts buried in the middle of a long context.

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.433
Look at what happens as a session grows.

8
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:29.300
Sending the full history, every call gets bigger and costlier.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.333
With compaction, the context stops growing once it hits a budget.

10
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.867
So you manage working memory deliberately, in three ways.

11
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.567
Window it: keep only the last N turns.

12
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.267
Summarize it: compress old turns into a running summary.

13
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:49.133
Or structure it: keep a compact scratchpad of facts instead of raw transcripts.

14
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.767
It helps to see the whole memory architecture.

15
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.333
Recorded experience is the base layer.

16
00:00:55.633 --> 00:00:57.100
Above it, durable facts.

17
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.572
And on top, volatile working memory.

18
00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:02.167
Different stores, different write policies.

19
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.100
Here is rolling summarization.

20
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.667
While the history is over budget, we act.

21
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:14.467
We take the oldest turns and ask the model to summarize them, keeping IDs, dates, and numbers verbatim.

22
00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:18.300
Then the summary is inserted at the front as earlier context.

23
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.867
Here is a real session.

24
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:28.333
The Orbital Research Agent collects forty NEO observations, tens of thousands of tokens of raw JSON.

25
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:34.500
Rolling compaction replaces the oldest with one line: checked fourteen asteroids, two hazardous.

26
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.400
Cost drops about seventy percent, and answers improve, because key facts stop getting lost.

27
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.369
Let's recap.

28
00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:44.933
Short term memory is the context window.

29
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:48.067
A long history costs both money and attention.

30
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.933
So window it, summarize it, or structure it.

31
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:53.663
And keep exact IDs and numbers.

32
00:01:53.663 --> 00:01:55.700
Next up: long term memory.
